BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:surels-place-residency-2027-winter@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Surel's Place: Month-Long Artist Residency (September 202
 6 deadline)
DESCRIPTION:Surel's Place is a residency in Garden City\, Idaho with a two-
 fold mission: to give artists the time\, shelter and professional support 
 their work deserves\, and to give the public accessible art experiences in
 cluding performances\, readings\, exhibits and workshops. In exchange for 
 the residency\, artists offer art experiences back to the community. OPEN 
 TO professional visual\, literary and performance artists: painters\, writ
 ers\, musicians\, architects\, filmmakers\, choreographers\, any artist wh
 o needs a place to focus. LIMITS: because of the property's constraints an
 d proximity to neighbours\, it cannot accommodate loud or very messy proce
 sses such as welding or blacksmithing. The organisers also note that predo
 minantly conceptual work\, work that must be explained to attract a viewer
 \, may not be met with as much enthusiasm by the jurors\, and that they we
 lcome artists who want to connect with an audience. Each cycle\, one of th
 e five residencies includes outreach to local schools through the PARTNER 
 Schools Program. REQUIREMENTS: maintain residence at the home and studio (
 no studio-use-only residencies)\; use the residency in a focused way towar
 ds a stated goal with no other employment or work happening simultaneously
 \; visual artists give a show of and/or lecture about their residency work
 \, writers give a reading and discussion session\; all artists offer at le
 ast one public workshop (2 to 3 hours\, minimal lecturing)\; visual artist
 s donate one piece of art to Surel's Place for future fundraising\; writer
 s allow Surel's Place to publish a free-standing excerpt of their residenc
 y work in books\, broadsides\, posters or similar. SALES COMMISSION applie
 s as set out in the funding notes. HOUSE RULES: the non-smoking requiremen
 t is non-negotiable and any evidence of smoking is grounds for early termi
 nation. Partners are welcome but children and pets cannot be accommodated\
 , no more than two adults may live there during a residency\, and partners
  wanting concurrent residencies must apply and be accepted separately. ELI
 GIBILITY: applicants must be 25 or older and non-smokers. APPLY by email t
 o info@surelsplace.org with the online Artist in Residence application plu
 s\, for VISUAL ARTISTS\, a cover letter introducing yourself and saying wh
 y you want to be at Surel's Place specifically\, a proposal including a wo
 rkshop outline\, a CV\, electronic images of 4 to 6 pieces of work and an 
 image list (title\, size\, medium\, year)\; for LITERARY applicants\, cove
 r letter\, proposal with workshop outline\, CV and PDF work samples with y
 our name and page number in the top right corner (fiction\, prose or scrip
 t no more than 10 typed double-spaced pages\; poetry no more than six poem
 s and no more than 10 pages)\; for OTHER MEDIUMS\, cover letter\, proposal
  with workshop outline\, CV and electronic samples (contact Surel's Place 
 to discuss sample requirements for your medium). Applications are reviewed
  by juries of accomplished artists\, writers and fine arts professionals\,
  and all applicants are notified by email.\n\nOrganisation: Surel's Place\
 nLocation: In person in Garden City\, Idaho\, USA (Boise area). Residents 
 must live at the home and studio\; no studio-only residencies.\nAward: Fre
 e rent\, utilities and wi-fi\, a living stipend of USD 100 per week\, a US
 D 300 travel stipend\, use of well-maintained bicycles\, free or discounte
 d tickets to art events and movies\, marketing through Surel's Place chann
 els\, and event hosting including logistics and installation help. Each re
 sident also gets a local host to orient them and host their public events.
  No application fee. NOTE: Surel's Place takes a commission on sales arisi
 ng from the residency\, 35% of retail price on artwork\, 45% on ticketed e
 vents that are offsite or need contracted services (calculated after admin
 istrative expenses)\, and 15% on books\, CDs or other merchandise tangenti
 al to the artist's medium. A surcharge may apply to cover additional utili
 ty costs if a partner joins.\nDuration: One month (longer may be possible 
 if justified in the application). The 1 September deadline covers residenc
 ies in January to May of the following year\, beginning four months after 
 the deadline\; the 1 March deadline covers July to November of the same ye
 ar.\n\nMore: https://surelsplace.org/month-long-residencies/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://surelsplace.org/month-long-residencies/?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person in Garden City\, Idaho\, USA (Boise area). Residents mus
 t live at the home and studio\; no studio-only residencies.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:erie-canal-museum-artist-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Erie Canal Museum: Erie Canal Artist-in-Residence 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Erie Canal Museum\, in partnership with the New York State 
 Canal Corporation\, is appointing two Erie Canal Artists-in-Residence for 
 a one-year\, year-long creative inquiry into the New York State Canal Syst
 em\, including the historic Erie Canal: reflecting on its past\, contempla
 ting its current condition\, and considering its future. The 2027 residenc
 ies are dedicated to projects that reflect upon the Canal's physical\, env
 ironmental and social condition and history\, its materiality and its cont
 ext\, and the project must directly address the Canal. Applicants propose 
 a specific creative visual project that must be exhibitable in a gallery s
 etting\, identify their target audiences and explain how the work will be 
 shared with them and with the public at large. Studies of Canal communitie
 s are encouraged\, but the Canal and its context must be central. Projects
  may focus on key areas or on the entire system\, and applicants are told 
 to weigh that scale: 524 miles of navigable waterways and 365 miles of mul
 ti-use trails. ELIGIBILITY: all artists are encouraged to apply\, and spec
 ial consideration goes to artists with ancestral ties to the Haudenosaunee
  Confederacy and/or the Mohican Nation and to artists who have lived or wo
 rked in Canal communities. Only individual artists or artist collectives m
 ay apply\; artists representing nonprofit or for-profit organisations may 
 not. Artists must be 18 or older and may not be currently enrolled in scho
 ol full time. For collectives\, only one USD 10\,000 stipend is awarded to
  the group. APPLICATION: a statement of interest and proposal (up to 500 w
 ords) covering why the residency interests you\, your proposed project and
  how it meets the three residency goals (engage with the Canal\, adjacent 
 Canalway Trails\, Canal Corp. and Museum staff\, Canal facilities\, commun
 ities and visitors\; reflect directly on the Canal's physical\, environmen
 tal and social condition and history\; consider a fuller\, more complete a
 ccount of the Canal's past\, present and future)\, which geographic sectio
 n you will focus on\, how you would share work publicly during the residen
 cy\, your proposed final project\, and how your previous work and public e
 ngagement relate\; an artist's statement (up to 300 words)\; a resume or C
 V (max three pages)\; two professional references with phone and email\; a
 nd ten examples of recent work (written work limited to three pages each) 
 with a list giving title\, date\, materials and size plus short descriptio
 ns. EVALUATION covers clarity and relevance of the proposal\, the scope\, 
 feasibility and clarity of the plan to reach the intended audience\, the p
 lan for public engagement including at least one public-facing event durin
 g the residency\, a final project usable in an exhibit and catalogue\, and
  the quality and relevance of prior work. Applications are reviewed by a p
 anel of Erie Canal Museum and Canal Corporation representatives plus invit
 ed arts professionals (the 2026 panel included Elizabeth Dubben of Collar 
 Works\, artist and educator Dennis RedMoon Darkeem\, and Garth Johnson\, C
 urator of Ceramics at the Everson Museum of Art)\; finalists interview wit
 h the panel. SUBMIT application packages as PDFs by email to Natalie Stets
 on\, Executive Director\, at employment@eriecanalmuseum.org with 'Erie Can
 al Artist-in-Residence' in the subject line\, by 11:59 pm on Sunday 6 Sept
 ember 2026. The RFP is at eriecanalmuseum.org/about/careers/.\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Erie Canal Museum (with the New York State Canal Corporation)\nLocati
 on: New York State\, USA. Non-residential: there is no live-in accommodati
 on\, and the work is fieldwork along the New York State Canal System (524 
 miles of navigable waterways and 365 miles of multi-use trails) and its co
 mmunities\, with engagement with Canal Corporation and Museum staff and fa
 cilities. The Museum is in the 1850 Syracuse Weighlock Building\, Syracuse
 \, NY. Finalist interviews are conducted virtually.\nAward: USD 10\,000 ar
 tist stipend per residency\, for two Artists-in-Residence. Collectives app
 lying as a group receive one USD 10\,000 stipend between them. No applicat
 ion fee. The final project feeds into an exhibit and catalogue\, with an e
 xhibit opening in the first half of 2028.\nDuration: One year: 1 January t
 o 31 December 2027. Finalist interviews 28 September to 23 October 2026 (v
 irtual)\; artist notification by 13 November 2026\; exhibit opening in the
  first half of 2028.\n\nMore: https://www.nyfa.org/opportunities/opportuni
 ty-info/?title=erie-canal-artist-in-residence&id=d469ab60-b5fd-41aa-ad27-a
 9fe0e841674&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.nyfa.org/opportunities/opportunity-info/?title=erie-canal-a
 rtist-in-residence&id=d469ab60-b5fd-41aa-ad27-a9fe0e841674&utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York State\, USA. Non-residential: there is no live-in accommo
 dation\, and the work is fieldwork along the New York State Canal System (
 524 miles of navigable waterways and 365 miles of multi-use trails) and it
 s communities\, with engagement with Canal Corporation and Museum staff an
 d facilities. The Museum is in the 1850 Syracuse Weighlock Building\, Syra
 cuse\, NY. Finalist interviews are conducted virtually.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:princeton-arts-fellowship-2027-2029@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: Princeton Arts Fellowship 2027-2029 (Lewis Center for the
  Arts)
DESCRIPTION:The Princeton Arts Fellowship places early-career artists at th
 e Lewis Center for the Arts for two academic years on a full-time salaried
  appointment\, on the conviction that exposure to the arts helps us make s
 ense of our lives and the lives of our neighbours. ELIGIBLE APPLICANTS are
  early-career composers\, conductors\, musicians\, choreographers\, playwr
 ights\, designers\, directors and performance artists. IMPORTANT FOR THIS 
 CYCLE: applications are accepted only for the Program in Dance\, the Progr
 am in Theater and Music Theater\, and the Department of Music. Fellows typ
 ically teach one undergraduate course per semester (subject to approval by
  the Office of the Dean of the Faculty)\, or in some cases take on an arti
 stic assignment with students in lieu of a class\; prior teaching experien
 ce is NOT required. Beyond that\, fellows are expected to engage with the 
 Princeton community through advising students\, talks\, workshops\, exhibi
 tions or other events\, and have time to pursue their own artistic practic
 e and scholarly research. NOT ELIGIBLE: the appointment cannot be used to 
 fund work leading to a PhD or any advanced degree\; holders of Princeton P
 hDs cannot apply\; past recipients of the Hodder Fellowship cannot apply\;
  and anyone with a sustained and continuous relationship with Princeton is
  excluded (an occasional and sporadic relationship is fine). Fellows may n
 ot hold concurrent teaching positions at other institutions during the app
 ointment. You may apply once per application cycle. APPLICATION via Interf
 olio: a CV\; a 750-word proposal broken into how you would use the fellows
 hip to develop your work (250 words)\, what you might teach or pursue with
  undergraduates (250 words)\, and your approach to building community with
  people of different backgrounds\, experiences\, perspectives and artistic
  practices through your practice\, teaching and/or research (250 words)\; 
 contact information for one reference\; and work samples with a 150-word s
 tatement on how they relate to your proposal\, submitted as links in a PDF
 . WORK SAMPLE SPECS: Dance (choreography)\, one to three links to performa
 nces\, rehearsals or research\, continuous footage only\, no trailers\, ph
 otographs or promotional material\, with cue points of no more than 3 to 4
  minutes per link and no more than 10 minutes total. Music\, one to two sc
 ores and/or links to performance examples totalling 10 minutes\, video opt
 ional. Theater\, up to 15 pages of writing sample and/or up to 10 design i
 mages (photos or renderings) and/or a video or audio reel with cue points\
 , no more than 10 minutes to watch. The deadline is 8 September 2026 at 11
 :59 pm EDT\; receipt of applications cannot be confirmed and late applicat
 ions are not accepted. FAQ and examples of building community in artistic 
 practice\, teaching and research: arts.princeton.edu/fellowships/faq.\n\nO
 rganisation: Princeton University\, Lewis Center for the Arts\nLocation: I
 n person on campus at Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA. Appointees are expecte
 d to be full and active members of the community with frequent\, engaged i
 nteraction with students. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship
  of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing.\nAward: USD 93\,000 salary
  per year for each of two academic years\, as a full-time 24-month appoint
 ment at the rank of Associate Professional Specialist. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Two academic years\, 2027-2028 and 2028-2029. Applications ope
 n 1 July 2026 and close 8 September 2026\; finalists attend an on-campus i
 nterview during the week of 7 December 2026.\n\nMore: https://apply.interf
 olio.com/187043?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://apply.interfolio.com/187043?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person on campus at Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA. Appointees ar
 e expected to be full and active members of the community with frequent\, 
 engaged interaction with students. These positions are not eligible for sp
 onsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:collar-works-elizabeth-murray-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Collar Works: Elizabeth Murray Artist Residency (EMAR) 20
 27
DESCRIPTION:The Elizabeth Murray Artist Residency gives emerging\, underrep
 resented and established artists an immersive\, communal atmosphere for ar
 t-making and dialogue on a 77-acre farm in Granville\, New York\, the clas
 sic farmhouse where the late Elizabeth Murray spent more than two decades 
 of summers and kept her studio in the large\, cathedral-like dairy barn. M
 urray\, who died in 2007\, won a Skowhegan Medal in Painting in 1986\, a M
 acArthur grant in 1999 and a MoMA career retrospective in 2005\; her husba
 nd Bob Holman is a poet and arts activist who founded the Bowery Poetry Cl
 ub. In 2017 the Murray-Holman family partnered with Collar Works\, a nonpr
 ofit contemporary arts organisation in Troy\, New York\, to build a summer
  residency carrying the legacy forward. EMAR is explicitly designed around
  the critical needs of emerging and underrepresented artists and of artist
 s with children\, with an active commitment to diversity\, equity\, access
 ibility and inclusion\, and describes itself as a safe space for creative 
 risk-taking\, personal growth and respite. Work is primarily in visual and
  literary arts\, though the programme supports new contemporary work acros
 s many art forms. TWO TRACKS: the INDIVIDUAL RESIDENCY takes up to five ar
 tists per session (including onsite artist-staff) for two weeks\, with 24-
 hour access to semi-private studios in the barn\, a private bedroom in the
  farmhouse\, shared bathrooms\, communal living room\, kitchen and a libra
 ry holding Elizabeth Murray's personal art book collection. The FAMILY RES
 IDENCY invites three to four artists and their families for one week\, wit
 h a private bedroom (families may need to room together)\, a semi-private 
 studio in the same barn\, and full-day childcare running as a parallel cre
 ative residency for children aged 5 to 12\, focused on art and nature prog
 ramming: while parent artists get uninterrupted studio time\, children wor
 k in their own EMAR Kids Art Studio\, explore outside through guided activ
 ities\, play\, swim\, read and take guided field trips to local farms and 
 museums. Partners are welcome to join and help with childcare\, but the pr
 ogramme is committed to fully supporting artists who want or need to atten
 d solo with their children\, and Collar Works works case by case with each
  artist to put the necessary support and equipment in place before arrival
  given the rural setting. APPLICATION requires artist information\, a stat
 ement of intent (250 to 500 words)\, an artist biography (250 words)\, ten
  examples of recent work (visual arts as JPEGs up to 2MB each\, with a PDF
  of links or a curated URL page for video and media works including any pa
 sswords\; literary arts as up to three poems totalling max six pages\, or 
 max ten pages of prose) and a list of works with title\, medium\, date and
  size. Applications go through Submittable and close 1 October 2026 (the S
 ubmittable listing shows a 2 October 2026 06:00 close in its own timezone)
 . Questions and technical assistance: info@collarworks.org.\n\nOrganisatio
 n: Collar Works\nLocation: In person on a 77-acre farm in Granville\, Wash
 ington County\, New York\, USA (the former summer home of Elizabeth Murray
  and Bob Holman). Collar Works itself is based in Troy\, New York.\nAward:
  Fully supported: there is no cost to participate for accepted artists. Pr
 ivate bedroom accommodation with shared bathrooms in the farmhouse\, 24-ho
 ur access to a semi-private studio in the barn that housed Elizabeth Murra
 y's studio\, all food provided (dinner cooked and eaten communally)\, and 
 use of the property including a swimming pool\, pond and 77 acres of field
 s and woods. Accepted artists also receive a USD 500 stipend to use at the
 ir discretion towards travel\, materials or other associated costs. The Fa
 mily Residency additionally provides free full-day (9am to 3pm) childcare 
 as a parallel creative residency for children aged 5 to 12\, plus a dedica
 ted Kids Art Studio. APPLICATION FEE: USD 35 (the first month of the appli
 cation period was free\; the fee applies from 1 August 2026).\nDuration: T
 wo weeks for individual artists\, one week for families. 2027 individual s
 essions: Session 1\, 15 to 29 May 2027\; Session 2\, 1 to 15 June 2027. 20
 27 family session: Session 3\, 19 to 26 June 2027. Finalist interviews Nov
 ember 2026\; final notification December 2026.\n\nMore: https://collarwork
 s.submittable.com/submit/360246/elizabeth-murray-artist-residency-2027?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://collarworks.submittable.com/submit/360246/elizabeth-murray-arti
 st-residency-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
LOCATION:In person on a 77-acre farm in Granville\, Washington County\, New
  York\, USA (the former summer home of Elizabeth Murray and Bob Holman). C
 ollar Works itself is based in Troy\, New York.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bica-generator-fund-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: BICA: Generator Fund 2026 (Kinetic and Potential Grants)
DESCRIPTION:The Generator Fund is BICA's regranting programme for visual ar
 tists in Buffalo and the surrounding two counties\, supported by the Andy 
 Warhol Foundation's Regional Regranting Program. It makes direct project g
 rants to artists rather than to institutions\, and welcomes professional\,
  emerging and experimental practitioners alike. CHOOSING A TRACK: Kinetic 
 Grants (up to USD 5\,000) suit a project with a clear beginning and end\, 
 built around a specific event\, release or presentation\, usually made alo
 ne or with a small team. Potential Grants (up to USD 10\,000) suit an ongo
 ing space\, system or collaborative structure that involves many people an
 d is meant to evolve over time\; these applications also need a sustainabi
 lity plan. ELIGIBILITY: you must live and work in Erie or Niagara County\,
  New York. You can apply as an individual artist\, an informal group or an
  unincorporated collective. NOT ELIGIBLE: 501(c)(3) organisations\; anyone
  receiving a solo exhibition honorarium from BICA in the same fiscal year\
 ; BICA employees\; and anyone who received a Potential Grant in the previo
 us round (they can still apply for a Kinetic Grant). The fund supports vis
 ual arts based projects\, including painting\, sculpture\, installation\, 
 photography\, social practice\, performance art\, video and zines\; projec
 ts rooted primarily in music\, theatre\, dance or literary arts without a 
 strong visual component are not eligible. APPLICATION runs through Submitt
 able and asks for a project title\, a 150-character tagline\, artistic con
 text (up to 250 words)\, a full project description (up to 500 words\, the
  most heavily weighted part\, expected to cover where the project happens 
 or how the public accesses it\, a timeline with key milestones\, the audie
 nce and how you will reach them\, and the people involved)\, a budget on B
 ICA's project budget sheet covering expenses\, in-kind support and income\
 , work samples (images\, video or PDFs of past work and a sense of what is
  to come) and a CV. SUPPORT before the deadline includes a virtual info se
 ssion on 12 August 2026 at 5:30 pm\, an in-person info session at BICA on 
 5 September 2026 at 3:30 pm (followed by a budget and CV workshop led by N
 ando Alvarez-Perez)\, one-on-one office hours with the Generator Fund coor
 dinator on Thursdays\, Fridays and Saturdays through August and September\
 , and group work sessions at BICA School Lab (30B Essex St) on 19 and 26 S
 eptember 2026\, 12 to 5 pm. Applications submitted a week or more early ma
 y receive feedback and be reopened for edits.\n\nOrganisation: Buffalo Ins
 titute for Contemporary Art (BICA)\nLocation: Applicants must live and wor
 k in Erie or Niagara County\, New York\, USA. BICA is at 30D Essex Street\
 , Buffalo\, NY 14213.\nAward: Two grant types: Kinetic Grants of up to USD
  5\,000 for short-term\, public-facing visual arts projects (exhibitions\,
  performances\, installations\, workshops\, publications)\, and Potential 
 Grants of up to USD 10\,000 for long-term\, collaborative or platform-buil
 ding projects (artist-run spaces\, recurring events\, media platforms and 
 other structures that extend beyond a single outcome). No application fee.
  The fund is part of the Andy Warhol Foundation's Regional Regranting Prog
 ram.\nDuration: Applications open 1 August 2026 and close 30 September 202
 6\; jurors review in October and meet in November\; grantees are notified 
 in December 2026\; cheques are handed over in January 2027 at the Meet the
  Grantees event\; proposed projects are to be completed by 31 December 202
 7.\n\nMore: https://www.generatorfund.org/apply?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.generatorfund.org/apply?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicants must live and work in Erie or Niagara County\, New York
 \, USA. BICA is at 30D Essex Street\, Buffalo\, NY 14213.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:beinecke-short-term-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Beinecke Library Short-Term Research Fellowships 2027-202
 8 (Yale)
DESCRIPTION:The Beinecke Library Fellowship Program offers competitive shor
 t-term fellowships to support research projects that substantively engage 
 with Yale Library Special Collections materials. WHO CAN APPLY: academic a
 nd independent scholars\, locally and globally\, with or without an instit
 utional affiliation\; the library explicitly welcomes applicants regardles
 s of institutional association\, race\, cultural background\, ability\, se
 xual orientation\, gender or socioeconomic status. Applications using trad
 itional archival and bibliographic research methods are encouraged\, and s
 o are creative\, interdisciplinary and non-traditional approaches to worki
 ng with the collections\, which makes this a live option for artists and w
 riters whose practice is research-driven. WHAT YOU GET: USD 5\,000 per mon
 th for an onsite research visit of between two weeks and two months\, paid
  at the start of the fellowship whenever possible (international fellows s
 hould expect payment to take several weeks)\; fellows pay their own taxes 
 on the award. TIMELINE: applications for the 2027-2028 awards opened 1 Jun
 e 2026 and close 15 August 2026\, via Interfolio\; all decisions are notif
 ied by 31 January 2027\; research must be conducted between 1 July 2027 an
 d 30 June 2028\, and awards cannot be deferred.\n\nOrganisation: Beinecke 
 Rare Book & Manuscript Library\, Yale University\nLocation: Onsite at Yale
  Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connecticut\, USA. Open to acad
 emic and independent scholars locally and globally\, regardless of institu
 tional association.\nAward: USD 5\,000 per month for research visits betwe
 en two weeks and two months. Funding is paid at the beginning of the fello
 wship where possible (payment to international fellows often takes several
  weeks)\; fellows are responsible for taxes on the award. Free to apply.\n
 Duration: Two weeks to two months of onsite research\, conducted between 1
  July 2027 and 30 June 2028\; awards cannot be deferred\n\nMore: https://b
 einecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/short-term?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://beinecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/shor
 t-term?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Onsite at Yale Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connectic
 ut\, USA. Open to academic and independent scholars locally and globally\,
  regardless of institutional association.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cpr-artist-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260824
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260825
SUMMARY:Deadline: CPR Center for Performance Research: Artist-in-Residence 
 Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:CPR's year-long Artist-in-Residence programme supports six New 
 York City-based artists working across contemporary dance\, performance an
 d time-based art. The organisation values experimental approaches to conte
 nt\, form and aesthetics and explicitly encourages risk-taking\, interdisc
 iplinarity and the unexpected\, so process-led and hard-to-categorise work
  is in scope rather than a liability. WHAT RESIDENTS RECEIVE: a $2\,000 st
 ipend\; 20 free hours of rehearsal space plus up to 150 further subsidised
  hours in CPR's two studios\, with priority and advance calendar access\; 
 participation in OPEN LAB (facilitated embodied workshops and feedback sho
 wings) and OPEN AiR (solo or shared presentations of current work)\; and a
 ccess to technical equipment and production support. ELIGIBILITY: artists 
 must be based in New York City and remain so throughout the cycle (no visa
  sponsorship)\; must have had work publicly presented before\; must not ha
 ve held a CPR residency in the last five years\; and current undergraduate
 s are not eligible. Individuals\, companies\, collaboratives and collectiv
 es may all apply\, each counting as a single residency. CPR strongly encou
 rages applications from BIPOC\, LGBTQ+\, immigrant and disabled artists an
 d from artists across generations. APPLICATION: a Google Form with narrati
 ve answers on the artist's central research focus and its alignment with C
 PR's values\; drafting answers in a separate document first is recommended
 . Selection is by an independent panel of artists\, curators and arts lead
 ers. Deadline: Monday 24 August 2026 at 5pm ET\; late submissions are not 
 accepted. Notifications November 2026. Questions: applications@cprnyc.org.
 \n\nOrganisation: CPR - Center for Performance Research\nLocation: Brookly
 n\, New York City\, USA. RESTRICTED: NYC-based artists only\; CPR does not
  sponsor visas\, and residents must remain NYC-based for the whole cycle.\
 nAward: $2\,000 stipend\, 20 free hours of rehearsal space\, up to 150 sub
 sidised rehearsal hours across CPR's two studios with priority and advance
  booking\, presentation slots in the OPEN LAB and OPEN AiR programmes\, an
 d access to technical equipment and production support. No application fee
 .\nDuration: One year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https://www.cprn
 yc.org/events/opencall-2027airprogram?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.cprnyc.org/events/opencall-2027airprogram?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Brooklyn\, New York City\, USA. RESTRICTED: NYC-based artists only
 \; CPR does not sponsor visas\, and residents must remain NYC-based for th
 e whole cycle.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:latitude-air-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: LATITUDE Artist in Residence 2027 (Chicago)
DESCRIPTION:The LATITUDE Artist in Residence program (started in 2013) is a
  one-month production residency where 8-10 artists are granted full access
  to LATITUDE's facilities and community to develop their practice. Its foc
 us is supporting artists who want to use LATITUDE's printing and scanning 
 services to expand their practice\, regardless of medium or experience\; p
 ast residents include photographers\, painters\, performance artists\, new
  media artists\, and sculptors. WHAT RESIDENTS RECEIVE: a $1\,000 printing
  stipend\, unlimited scanning\, a personal studio with a calibrated workst
 ation and 24/7 access\, training and guidance on tech from staff\, an orga
 nised public event to present new work\, and lifetime free lab access. LAT
 ITUDE does not offer a housing stipend but will help schedule studio visit
 s during the month. PUBLIC PROGRAMMING: during the residency each artist p
 uts on one community programming event (past examples include panel discus
 sions\, artist talks\, performances\, workshops\, and critiques\; a galler
 y space is available for curation) and is asked to act as a mentor to the 
 community\, sharing insights and creative methods. WHO SHOULD APPLY: artis
 ts interested in production\, education\, and tech experimentation\; no pr
 ior printing experience\, art education\, or media specificity is required
 \, though professionalism and communication are expected. 2027 PANEL COMMI
 TTEE: Yechen Zhao\, B. Ingrid Olson\, Teri Carson. TIMELINE: applications 
 open 1 August 2026 (free before 16 August\, a $20 donation thereafter) and
  close 1 September 2026 at 12pm (noon) CST\; the selection committee revie
 ws in September\, meets to decide finalists in October\, and acceptances a
 re sent early November 2026. Apply at latitudechicago.org/artist-in-reside
 nce.\n\nOrganisation: LATITUDE Chicago\nLocation: Chicago\, Illinois\, USA
 . No housing stipend offered.\nAward: One-month production residency for 8
 -10 artists: a $1\,000 printing stipend\, unlimited scanning\, a personal 
 studio with a calibrated workstation\, staff training and guidance on tech
 \, an organised public event\, and lifetime free lab access. No housing st
 ipend. Free to apply before 16 August 2026\; a $20 donation thereafter.\nD
 uration: One month (residency takes place in 2027)\n\nMore: https://www.la
 titudechicago.org/artist-in-residence?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.latitudechicago.org/artist-in-residence?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Chicago\, Illinois\, USA. No housing stipend offered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:onview-artist-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: ON::View Artist Residency Program 2027 (Savannah\, Georgi
 a)
DESCRIPTION:Located in the heart of Savannah's Starland District at ARTS So
 utheast\, the fully-funded ON::View Artist Residency provides a free\, hig
 h-visibility studio space\, lodging\, and a $500 stipend for an artist or 
 curator to complete a new project\, continue an in-progress endeavour\, or
  conduct research. The studio's large east-facing street-level windows loo
 k onto Bull Street\, allowing the resident's work to be on view to the com
 munity 24/7 as visitors and passersby witness the process from concept to 
 final execution. It is the only residency of its kind in Savannah and welc
 omes creatives from around the globe working in all media. STUDIO: 12 x 12
  feet just off the ARTS Southeast lobby\, with 24-hour access\; residents 
 must work during public open hours (Thursday to Sunday\, 12 to 5PM) a mini
 mum of one day per week\, and must be present during the First Friday Prog
 ram Finale. Restrictions: no aerosol or toxic/high-VOC materials\, no ongo
 ing or repetitive loud noise (occasional hand and power tool use is allowe
 d)\; accepted applicants provide their own materials and supplies. LODGING
 : the 5th Dimension Apartment a few blocks away\, a cozy studio with full 
 kitchen and bath\, private gated entrance and garden\, wifi and TV\, queen
  bed\; houses up to 2 people (larger teams should arrange additional housi
 ng). No smoking or pets. ENGAGEMENT: residents do an Instagram takeover vi
 a @onviewresidency\, are encouraged to give a public lecture\, workshop\, 
 or interactive event\, and may participate in an annual group exhibition i
 n February 2028. ELIGIBILITY: creatives in all media\; collaborative teams
  are eligible (one proposal per team)\; current Studio Members or those wh
 o were Studio Members in the last 6 months are not eligible. Creatives may
  seek additional funding from other organisations\, and ARTS Southeast wil
 l provide a letter of support to accepted residents seeking grants. KEY DA
 TES: deadline Monday 7 September 2026 by 11:59PM\; first-round notificatio
 ns 18 September 2026\; interviews 21 to 25 September 2026\; final notifica
 tions 12 October 2026. APPLICATION: pay the $50 application fee (waivable 
 at ARTS Southeast's discretion for undue burden)\, then complete the onlin
 e application. Supported in part by the Georgia Council for the Arts\, the
  National Endowment for the Arts\, and the City of Savannah.\n\nOrganisati
 on: ARTS Southeast\nLocation: Starland District\, Savannah\, Georgia\, USA
 . Open to creatives from around the globe.\nAward: Fully funded: free high
 -visibility studio space\, free lodging at the 5th Dimension Apartment\, a
 nd a $500 stipend (for travel\, materials\, or any use). $50 application f
 ee\, waivable at the organisation's discretion if it is an undue burden.\n
 Duration: February to May 2027\n\nMore: https://artssoutheast.org/onview-r
 esidency-app?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://artssoutheast.org/onview-residency-app?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Starland District\, Savannah\, Georgia\, USA. Open to creatives fr
 om around the globe.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:warhol-foundation-exhibition-support-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Andy Warhol Foundation: Exhibition Support (Fall 2026 rou
 nd)
DESCRIPTION:Supports solo\, two-person and thematic group exhibitions of co
 ntemporary visual art. The Foundation explicitly prioritises artists whose
  work is under-recognised and whose practices are experimental or hard to 
 categorise\, which makes it a genuine route for media arts\, digital cultu
 re and art-and-science exhibitions rather than a blue-chip-only fund. Gran
 ts of USD 60\,000 to 100\,000 cover direct exhibition costs including arti
 st fees\, new commissions\, catalogue production and artist-designed publi
 c programming\, but not staff salaries. Applicants may request no more tha
 n 25 percent of total direct costs. The proposal is a roughly three-page c
 uratorial letter plus detailed direct-cost budget\, organisational budget 
 and IRS documentation. Deadlines are 1 March and 1 September annually\, wi
 th notifications 1 July and 1 January. IMPORTANT: this is a postmark or em
 ail-timestamp deadline\, not a portal deadline. Fiscal sponsors are explic
 itly not accepted\; the applicant must hold its own IRS ruling letter. The
  six-month-to-two-year timing rule is strict\, so a September 2026 applica
 tion suits exhibitions programmed from mid-2027 onward.\n\nOrganisation: T
 he Andy Warhol Foundation for the Visual Arts\nLocation: Applicant's own v
 enue. US-based 501(c)(3) required\; international organisations must first
  submit a letter of inquiry before any proposal is accepted.\nAward: USD 6
 0\,000 to 100\,000. Direct costs only (artist payments\, catalogue and pub
 lication\, artist-designed public programmes)\; staff salaries excluded. R
 equest must not exceed 25 percent of total direct project costs\, which fu
 nctions as a de facto 75 percent co-financing requirement. No application 
 fee.\nDuration: Exhibition must open between 6 months and 2 years after no
 tification.\n\nMore: https://warholfoundation.org/grants/application-guide
 lines/exhibition-support/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://warholfoundation.org/grants/application-guidelines/exhibition-s
 upport/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicant's own venue. US-based 501(c)(3) required\; international
  organisations must first submit a letter of inquiry before any proposal i
 s accepted.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:warhol-foundation-multiyear-program-support-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Andy Warhol Foundation: Multi-year Program Support (Fall 
 2026 round)
DESCRIPTION:This is the closest thing in US foundation philanthropy to core
  support for an organisation running an ongoing public programme\, as oppo
 sed to a single exhibition. It funds a two-year slate of exhibitions\, res
 idencies\, public art\, screenings\, performances\, lectures and publicati
 ons\, which means a recurring festival or convening series can be underwri
 tten here. Grants are USD 60\,000 to 100\,000 over two years and may not e
 xceed 25 percent of annual operating budget. The Foundation states a prefe
 rence for organisations supporting artists with experimental\, hard-to-cat
 egorise practices\, putting digital culture\, sound art and art-and-scienc
 e programmers squarely in scope. Applications require a forward programme 
 schedule covering at least the twelve months after the award\, a three-pag
 e narrative focused specifically on how artists benefit (the stated centra
 l evaluation criterion)\, a full one-year organisational budget and 501(c)
 (3) documentation. Deadlines are 1 March and 1 September\, notifications 1
  July and 1 January. Fiscal sponsors are not accepted. Organisations that 
 received a Multi-year grant within the past year are ineligible\, and prop
 osals centred on anniversaries\, relocations or major renovations are expl
 icitly discouraged.\n\nOrganisation: The Andy Warhol Foundation for the Vi
 sual Arts\nLocation: Programming delivered by the applicant organisation. 
 US 501(c)(3) required\; international organisations must submit a letter o
 f inquiry first.\nAward: USD 60\,000 to 100\,000 across two years. Request
  must not exceed 25 percent of the organisation's annual operating budget.
  No formal match\, but the 25 percent cap means the balance must come from
  elsewhere. No application fee.\nDuration: Two years of programming\, begi
 nning after the 1 January 2027 notification.\n\nMore: https://warholfounda
 tion.org/grants/application-guidelines/multi-year-program-support/?utm_sou
 rce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://warholfoundation.org/grants/application-guidelines/multi-year-p
 rogram-support/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Programming delivered by the applicant organisation. US 501(c)(3) 
 required\; international organisations must submit a letter of inquiry fir
 st.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:warhol-foundation-small-scale-project-grants-2026@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Andy Warhol Foundation: Project Grants for Small-Scale Or
 ganizations (Fall 2026 round)
DESCRIPTION:Launched in February 2026 as an expansion of the Foundation's p
 rogramme to reach organisations too small for the Exhibition Support and M
 ulti-year strands\, and it is the single most relevant new US scheme for s
 mall media-arts festivals. It funds visual arts projects with a public com
 ponent\, explicitly naming film festivals\, exhibitions\, installations\, 
 residencies and interdisciplinary collaborations. Grants are USD 20\,000 t
 o 30\,000 toward direct costs. The hard gate is the USD 200\,000 annual op
 erating budget ceiling plus US 501(c)(3) status held directly by the appli
 cant\, not via a fiscal sponsor. The Foundation states it assesses creativ
 e risk-taking and the growth of participating artists rather than communit
 y impact metrics\, which suits experimental sound and digital culture prog
 ramming. Proposals are a maximum of three pages plus a one-year operating 
 budget\, a project budget and IRS documentation. Deadlines are 1 March (pr
 ojects begin 1 July or later) and 1 September (projects begin 1 January or
  later). Youth-focused projects are ineligible. One project per round\, an
 d grantees must wait a year after project completion and final reporting b
 efore reapplying.\n\nOrganisation: The Andy Warhol Foundation for the Visu
 al Arts\nLocation: Applicant's own venue or programme\, with a mandatory p
 ublic component. US-based only.\nAward: USD 20\,000 to 30\,000 toward dire
 ct costs such as artist payments and production. No application fee.\nDura
 tion: Project must complete within one year of notification\; September-ro
 und projects may begin 1 January 2027 or later.\n\nMore: https://warholfou
 ndation.org/grants/application-guidelines/project-grants-for-small-scale-o
 rganizations/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://warholfoundation.org/grants/application-guidelines/project-gran
 ts-for-small-scale-organizations/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicant's own venue or programme\, with a mandatory public compo
 nent. US-based only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:teiger-foundation-hosting-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: Teiger Foundation: Hosting Grant (September 2026 quarterl
 y deadline)
DESCRIPTION:Funds US organisations to present curator-led contemporary visu
 al art exhibitions and projects that originated elsewhere\, on condition t
 he presenting institution meaningfully adapts the project to its own conte
 xt. Teiger's remit covers group exhibitions\, single-artist surveys\, part
 icipatory and community-engaged projects\, digital exhibitions\, and live 
 and virtual performance\, making it one of very few US funders that will d
 irectly finance media-art and performance programming at organisational le
 vel. Requests are fixed at USD 50\,000 or USD 75\,000 with up to 30 percen
 t for indirect costs\, and there is no minimum organisation budget size\, 
 with smaller organisations explicitly favoured for infrastructure support.
  Hosting deadlines are quarterly: 9 September 2026 and 9 December 2026\, b
 oth 17:00 ET\, each mapped to a defined project-opening window roughly a y
 ear out. IMPORTANT: re-presenting a touring project unchanged is explicitl
 y not competitive\; the curatorial adaptation is the substance of the appl
 ication. US 501(c)(3) status is required and the fund is not open to indiv
 iduals or non-US organisations. Teiger's larger biennial Call for Proposal
 s is not running in 2026 and next opens in autumn 2026 with an early-2027 
 deadline.\n\nOrganisation: Teiger Foundation\nLocation: United States. The
  presenting organisation must be US-based\, though the originating project
  may have come from anywhere in the world.\nAward: USD 50\,000 or USD 75\,
 000 (fixed request tiers\, applicant chooses). Up to 30 percent may cover 
 indirect and overhead costs. Organisations with annual budgets under USD 3
 .5 million receive special consideration for infrastructure costs. No matc
 hing requirement. No application fee.\nDuration: Roughly 12 months\; the 9
  September 2026 deadline covers projects opening between 1 April 2027 and 
 31 March 2028.\n\nMore: https://teigerfoundation.org/what-we-fund-how-to-a
 pply/hosting?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://teigerfoundation.org/what-we-fund-how-to-apply/hosting?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States. The presenting organisation must be US-based\, thou
 gh the originating project may have come from anywhere in the world.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:wave-hill-van-lier-ankhlave-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: Wave Hill / AnkhLave Arts Alliance: 2027 Van Lier Fellows
 hip (NYC)
DESCRIPTION:Wave Hill\, in partnership with AnkhLave Arts Alliance\, invite
 s NYC-based artists who identify as BIPOC to apply for the 2027 Van Lier F
 ellowship as part of the 9th Annual AnkhLave Garden Project at Wave Hill P
 ublic Garden and Cultural Center in the Bronx. The project supports artist
 s who draw on research\, scholarship and lived knowledge to present site-r
 esponsive work at the intersection of nature\, culture and place\, engagin
 g with Wave Hill's plant collections\, history\, design and architecture. 
 The Van Lier Fellowship (funded by the Edward and Sally Van Lier Fund thro
 ugh the New York Community Trust) will be awarded to two NYC-based visual 
 artists aged 30 or younger. FELLOWSHIP: participation in the Winter Worksp
 ace residency (23 February - 18 April 2027)\; a $10\,435 stipend including
  an $8\,000 honorarium and transportation stipend\; a solo exhibition in t
 he Sunroom or Sun Porch (15 May - 4 July 2027)\; public engagement through
  Winter Workspace programming and a Meet the Artist program\; mentorship w
 ith Wave Hill curatorial staff\, working artists and arts professionals\; 
 and AnkhLave Alumni Network benefits. ELIGIBILITY: emerging artists 30 or 
 younger (born 1997 or after) from historically underrepresented communitie
 s\; live in one of the five NYC boroughs\; demonstrate financial need\; ha
 ve completed college and not be enrolled in a degree-granting program duri
 ng the residency\; not represented by a commercial gallery\; available for
  both programs\; cannot have been a Van Lier Fellow elsewhere or have prev
 iously done a Winter Workspace residency or exhibited at Wave Hill or with
  AnkhLave. Works may not include nudity\, depictions of violence or profan
 ity. Artists may apply to only one of the two 2027 AnkhLave Garden Project
  opportunities. Questions: visualarts@wavehill.org with '2027 Van Lier-Ank
 hlave' in the subject. DEADLINE: 10 August 2026. Apply via SlideRoom.\n\nO
 rganisation: Wave Hill / AnkhLave Arts Alliance\nLocation: Wave Hill Publi
 c Garden and Cultural Center\, the Bronx\, New York City\, USA. For NYC-ba
 sed BIPOC visual artists aged 30 or younger.\nAward: $10\,435 stipend\, in
 cluding an $8\,000 honorarium\, a transportation stipend for the residency
 \, and professional development opportunities\, plus the Winter Workspace 
 residency\, a solo exhibition with public programs\, mentorship from Wave 
 Hill curatorial staff and arts professionals\, and AnkhLave Alumni Network
  benefits. Two fellowships awarded. No application fee.\nDuration: January
  to December 2027\; Winter Workspace residency 23 February - 18 April 2027
 \; solo exhibition 15 May - 4 July 2027\n\nMore: https://wavehill.slideroo
 m.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral#/login/pr
 ogram/89300
URL:https://wavehill.slideroom.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral#/login/program/89300
LOCATION:Wave Hill Public Garden and Cultural Center\, the Bronx\, New York
  City\, USA. For NYC-based BIPOC visual artists aged 30 or younger.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:wave-hill-sunroom-project-space-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260810
SUMMARY:Deadline: Wave Hill: 2027 Sunroom Project Space (NYC)
DESCRIPTION:The Sunroom Project Space gives artists in the New York City ar
 ea who are in the early stages of their careers\, regardless of age\, the 
 chance to develop and show a site-specific solo project in the Sunroom or 
 Sun Porch of Glyndor Gallery at Wave Hill. For the 2027 season\, Wave Hill
  is especially interested in projects that recognise ancestral wisdom in r
 elation to our connection with the earth and soil: practices\, beliefs and
  values passed down through generations\, particularly those that foster h
 armonious relationships with our ecosystems\, for example regenerative far
 ming and agricultural practices and the care of soil\, water and plant lif
 e. Projects engaging the history of earth or land art (concepts\, material
 s\, sites and relationships to place) that intersect with sustainable prac
 tices and ancestral wisdom are also welcome. AWARD: four artists are selec
 ted for solo presentations\, each receiving a $2\,000 honorarium\, exhibit
 ion and professional development support\, and nine to twelve months to re
 search and develop their ideas. Each project is accompanied by a Meet the 
 Artist program co-designed with the artist\, and a short exhibition essay 
 published in print and online. ELIGIBILITY: artists working across media\,
  in the early stages of their career regardless of education\, living with
 in a 50-mile radius of the Bronx. Not eligible: students enrolled in a deg
 ree-granting program\; artists with commercial gallery representation\; ar
 tists who have previously exhibited a site-specific project at Wave Hill\;
  artists who participated in the 2026 Winter Workspace program at Wave Hil
 l. Applications are reviewed by a jury of Wave Hill's Curator of Visual Ar
 ts\, an invited guest curator and a prior 2026 Sunroom Project Space artis
 t. An online information session is held Monday 13 July\, 12-1PM\; RSVP to
  visualarts@wavehill.org with the subject line '2027 Sunroom Project Space
  Info Session'. DEADLINE: 9 August 2026. Apply via SlideRoom.\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Wave Hill\nLocation: Glyndor Gallery\, Wave Hill\, the Bronx\, New Yo
 rk City\, USA. For early-career artists living within a 50-mile radius of 
 the Bronx.\nAward: $2\,000 honorarium per artist\, plus exhibition and pro
 fessional development support\, a co-designed Meet the Artist public progr
 am\, and a short exhibition essay published in print and online. Four arti
 sts selected. No application fee.\nDuration: Nine to twelve months of rese
 arch and development leading to a solo presentation during the 2027 season
 \n\nMore: https://wavehill.slideroom.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral#/login/program/89416
URL:https://wavehill.slideroom.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral#/login/program/89416
LOCATION:Glyndor Gallery\, Wave Hill\, the Bronx\, New York City\, USA. For
  early-career artists living within a 50-mile radius of the Bronx.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:james-castle-house-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: James Castle House: 2027 Artist Residencies (Boise\, Idah
 o)
DESCRIPTION:The James Castle House in Boise\, Idaho (the preserved home of 
 self-taught artist James Castle) opens applications for its 2027 residency
  terms from 16 July to 28 August 2026. TWO-MONTH RESIDENCY: an immersive f
 ocus on a project or body of work responding to the history and legacy of 
 James Castle or the site\; open to artists\, scholars\, creators and profe
 ssionals in any field\; $4\,000 stipend\; residents share their practice t
 hrough public events (workshops\, open studios\, presentations) and donate
  a selected piece to the City of Boise collection. Terms: 1 March - 30 Apr
 il\, 1 June - 31 July\, or 1 September - 31 October 2027. TWO-WEEK RESIDEN
 CY: time and space for a single site-responsive project\; $1\,000 stipend\
 ; one public event. Terms: 11-25 January or 1-15 December 2027. A rolling 
 PROJECT RESIDENCY also considers proposals case by case (email jamescastle
 house@cityofboise.org). ELIGIBILITY: 18+\, legally able to work in the Uni
 ted States\, able to pass a background check\; emerging and mid-career pra
 ctitioners in any field\, though the property cannot accommodate loud or e
 specially messy processes. Residents live alone on site. Selection by comm
 ittee on strength of proposal\, application\, work samples and references.
  Virtual information session 21 July 2026\, 1pm MT. Applicants notified Se
 ptember 2026. DEADLINE: 28 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: James Castle Hous
 e / City of Boise\nLocation: Boise\, Idaho\, USA. Open to applicants legal
 ly able to work in the United States.\nAward: $4\,000 stipend for two-mont
 h terms or $1\,000 for two-week terms\, to assist with living expenses and
  supplies\, plus solo live-in accommodation and studio at the historic Jam
 es Castle House. Residents donate one selected piece to the City of Boise 
 collection at the end of the term. No application fee.\nDuration: Two-mont
 h terms (1 Mar - 30 Apr\, 1 Jun - 31 Jul\, 1 Sep - 31 Oct 2027) or two-wee
 k terms (11-25 Jan and 1-15 Dec 2027)\n\nMore: https://www.jamescastlehous
 e.org/residencies/apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=
 referral
URL:https://www.jamescastlehouse.org/residencies/apply/?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Boise\, Idaho\, USA. Open to applicants legally able to work in th
 e United States.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jcal-artworks-fellowship-2026-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260713
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260714
SUMMARY:Deadline: JCAL: ARTWorks Fellowship 2026-2027
DESCRIPTION:Jamaica Center for Arts and Learning (JCAL) invites New York Ci
 ty-based visual artists to apply for ARTWorks\, its Artist Fellowship Trai
 ning Seminar Series: a six-month fellowship focused on career sustainabili
 ty that amplifies the work of emerging\, underrepresented visual artists. 
 Six Fellows treat their practice as a small business\, set entrepreneurial
  and professional goals\, and build a long-term plan\, supported by a Prog
 ram Manager\, an established Artist Mentor\, cohort meetings\, and at leas
 t six seminar events (past topics: copyright and contracts\, working with 
 galleries and collectors\, grant writing\, taxes and accounting\, marketin
 g\, art handling). SUPPORT: $5\,000 per Fellow paid over the program\, fre
 e shared studio space (about 550 sq ft\, as available) with a storage clos
 et\, one free JCAL class\, and a concluding two-month group exhibition wit
 h catalogue (opens 9 April 2027). ELIGIBILITY: reside in New York City (pr
 eferably Queens) or Long Island\; identify as BIIPOC (Black\, Immigrant\, 
 Indigenous\, People of Color)\; demonstrate clarity of concept and strong 
 craft with a deep commitment to social practice\; must NOT have received m
 ajor exposure via exhibitions\, grants or residencies\; must not be enroll
 ed in any college degree or certificate program\; past Fellows ineligible.
  Priority to Queens-based artists of underrepresented communities\; multid
 isciplinary practices welcome\, but the program focuses on visual artists 
 working in traditional forms. APPLY via Submittable with six images OR two
  3-5 minute videos\, a one-page work-sample description\, artist statement
  (max 300 words)\, one professional reference (max 300 words)\, CV\, and a
  paragraph on your expectations of ARTWorks. Jury reviews submissions with
 out obvious identifiers. TIMELINE: first-round notifications 3 August 2026
 \, interviews 11-14 August\, Fellows notified 21 August\, program runs 7 O
 ctober 2026 to 18 June 2027. DEADLINE: midnight Monday 13 July 2026 (porta
 l closes 14 July 2026\, 6:00 AM ET).\n\nOrganisation: Jamaica Center for A
 rts and Learning (JCAL)\nLocation: Jamaica\, Queens\, New York City\, USA.
  Open to visual artists based in New York City (preferably Queens) or Long
  Island\; priority to Queens-based BIIPOC artists.\nAward: $5\,000 per Fel
 low\, paid out over the course of the program\, plus free shared studio sp
 ace at JCAL (about 550 sq ft\, as available)\, a 7 x 4 ft storage closet\,
  one free JCAL class (space-available)\, an Artist Mentor and seminar seri
 es\, and a concluding two-month group exhibition with catalogue in JCAL's 
 Miller and Community galleries. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-month f
 ellowship: 7 October 2026 to 18 June 2027\; exhibition opens 9 April 2027\
 n\nMore: https://jcal.submittable.com/submit/354501/artworks-fellowship-20
 26-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
