UID:adrianna-newell-fellowship-artsrec-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Adrianna Newell Fellowship at Arts&Rec 2026
DESCRIPTION:Inaugural Adrianna Newell Fellowship\, a live/work studio resid
 ency in Stamford\, NY (Western Catskills) for October 2026\, with possible
  extension into November. Named for board member Adrianna Newell\, it invi
 tes queer lens-based artists working in photography and moving image with 
 an active\, research-driven or conceptually rich practice\, especially wor
 k engaging queer perspectives on land\, rurality and regional space\, or q
 ueer identity\, memory\, intimacy\, community and embodiment. Open to arti
 sts from everywhere. Fellows receive a 1000 sq ft studio with attached apa
 rtment\, a $1\,500 combined materials stipend and per diem\, and introduct
 ions to the local creative community\; an open-studio presentation\, artis
 t talk or public exhibition at the end is required. Travel\, especially in
 ternational\, is not funded (residents are encouraged to seek travel suppo
 rt elsewhere). Apply by emailing a single PDF (one-page description of you
 r work and planned project\, 10 to 20 work samples with short descriptions
 \, and a current CV) to programs@artsrec.org. Deadline: 31 July 2026\; not
 ices by end of August.\n\nOrganisation: Arts&Rec Inc\nLocation: Stamford\,
  NY (Western Catskills)\, USA\nAward: Live/work residency: use of a 1000 s
 q ft studio and attached apartment\, plus a $1\,500 total materials stipen
 d and per diem. Travel (especially international) is not funded. No applic
 ation fee.\nDuration: October 2026 (four weeks to two months\, based on pr
 oject needs\; possible extension into November)\n\nMore: https://www.nyfa.
 org/opportunities/opportunity-info/?title=call-for-applications%3A-the-adr
 ianna-newell-fellowship-at-arts%26rec%2C-october-2026&id=013e03ad-6edf-47f
 b-8d14-291bdc930a31&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
URL:https://www.nyfa.org/opportunities/opportunity-info/?title=call-for-app
 lications%3A-the-adrianna-newell-fellowship-at-arts%26rec%2C-october-2026&
 id=013e03ad-6edf-47fb-8d14-291bdc930a31&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Stamford\, NY (Western Catskills)\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cafe-royal-performance-grant-summer-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260803
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260804
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cafe Royal Cultural Foundation: Performance Grant (Summer
  2026)
DESCRIPTION:The Cafe Royal Cultural Foundation NYC Performance Grant suppor
 ts individual artists producing theatre\, dance and performance art (which
  may be accompanied by music) moving toward a public performance. The Summ
 er 2026 cycle offers up to $10\,000 for eligible production costs: actors\
 , dancers and guest performers\, director/choreographer/composer and creat
 ive-team fees\, costumes\, lighting\, sound\, scenic\, props and set\, reh
 earsal and venue rental\, and production and technical staff. Funds do not
  cover the creation or composition of new work\; if the performance is a p
 lay it must be a full production (readings do not qualify). ELIGIBILITY: i
 ndividual performers only (not groups or organizations) who are US citizen
 s or resident aliens\, current NYC residents who have lived in the city at
  least one year and will remain through project completion. This cycle acc
 epts only 40 applications and closes early if that cap is reached. Applica
 nts may submit up to 5 video links. Apply via the foundation's performance
  page. Deadline: 3 August 2026\, 9:00 am ET (or earlier if 40 applications
  are received).\n\nOrganisation: Cafe Royal Cultural Foundation NYC\nLocat
 ion: New York City (five boroughs)\, or a location in close proximity to N
 YC approved by the foundation\, USA\nAward: Up to $10\,000 for production 
 costs (performers\, collaborators\, rehearsal and venue rental\, design el
 ements\, technical staff\, etc.). Does not fund the creation or compositio
 n of new work. No application fee.\nDuration: Production grant\; performan
 ce must start no sooner than 1 November 2026 and complete by 22 September 
 2027\n\nMore: https://caferoyalculturalfoundation.org/performance-page?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://caferoyalculturalfoundation.org/performance-page?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York City (five boroughs)\, or a location in close proximity t
 o NYC approved by the foundation\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:queens-museum-qm-jerome-emerging-artist-fellowship-2027-2028@artificial
 nouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Queens Museum: QM-Jerome Foundation Fellowship for Emergi
 ng Artists 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:The QM-Jerome Foundation Fellowship for Emerging Artists select
 s two emerging visual artists living in New York City for its 2027-2028 co
 hort. Each fellow receives a $20\,000 award\, professional development con
 sultations\, mentorship from Queens Museum staff and an established New Yo
 rk-based artist\, and a solo exhibition at the Queens Museum in 2028\, wit
 h curatorial support across a full year of project development. Aimed at e
 arly-career visual artists (roughly two to ten years of professional pract
 ice) who have a consistent practice and developing public profile but have
  not yet reached major institutional recognition. ELIGIBILITY: must curren
 tly reside in one of NYC's five boroughs and maintain residency through th
 e fellowship\; artists enrolled in degree-granting programs are not eligib
 le\; review the detailed criteria on exhibition history\, previous grants 
 and years of practice. Supported by the Jerome Foundation. Apply via the Q
 ueens Museum fellowship program page. Deadline: 28 September 2026\, 11:59 
 PM EST.\n\nOrganisation: Queens Museum (with the Jerome Foundation)\nLocat
 ion: Queens\, New York City\, USA\nAward: $20\,000 fellowship award per ar
 tist (two artists selected)\, plus professional development consultations\
 , mentorship from Queens Museum staff and an established New York-based ar
 tist\, and a solo exhibition at the Queens Museum in 2028. No application 
 fee.\nDuration: One-year fellowship (2027-2028)\, culminating in a 2028 so
 lo exhibition\n\nMore: https://queensmuseum.org/fellowship/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://queensmuseum.org/fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Queens\, New York City\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:moorhead-call-public-art-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
SUMMARY:Deadline: City of Moorhead: 2026 Call for Public Art (Movable Inter
 active Piece)
DESCRIPTION:The City of Moorhead is seeking proposals for an inspirational\
 , movable public artwork to be installed at rotating locations across the 
 city. Interactive elements are explicitly encouraged\, with kinetic sculpt
 ure\, digital installation and tactile elements named as examples. Strong 
 proposals invite public participation\, promote creativity and youth engag
 ement\, are safe for all members of the public\, are durable enough for ye
 ar-round outdoor conditions\, and draw people to gather. Submissions are r
 eviewed and ranked by the Artist Review Team\, whose recommendation passes
  to the Art and Culture Commission and then to City Council for the final 
 decision. Questions go to art@moorheadmn.gov. Proposals are due by 10:00am
  on Friday 4 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: City of Moorhead\, Minnesota
 \nLocation: Moorhead\, Minnesota\, USA. The piece will be sited at differe
 nt locations throughout the city. No geographic eligibility restriction is
  stated in the call\, so non-US artists appear eligible\, but this is not 
 confirmed in writing.\nAward: Not published. The call announcement states 
 no budget\, commission amount or artist fee\, and the linked Request for A
 rt document could not be retrieved. Confirm the budget with art@moorheadmn
 .gov before investing time in a proposal. No application fee is mentioned.
 \nDuration: Single commission\; timeline not published\n\nMore: https://ww
 w.moorheadmn.gov/news/posts/2026-call-for-public-art/?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.moorheadmn.gov/news/posts/2026-call-for-public-art/?utm_sou
 rce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Moorhead\, Minnesota\, USA. The piece will be sited at different l
 ocations throughout the city. No geographic eligibility restriction is sta
 ted in the call\, so non-US artists appear eligible\, but this is not conf
 irmed in writing.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:penn-college-gallery-exhibitions-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Gallery at Penn College: 2027-28 Call for Exhibitions
DESCRIPTION:The Gallery at Penn College is accepting proposals for solo\, t
 wo-person and group exhibitions in its 2027-28 season. All entries are jur
 ied through a blind selection process by a gallery committee of faculty\, 
 staff\, K-12 art educators and community artists. The space is 3\,000 squa
 re feet with 82 to 250 running feet of linear wall depending on configurat
 ion and a ceiling height of 11 feet 4 inches\, and all exhibitions are ins
 talled by gallery staff. Site-specific installations requiring artist inst
 allation need an approved planning document first. Campus visits are a val
 ued part of programming though some exhibitions are scheduled without one\
 ; visiting artists give a 20 to 30 minute gallery talk at the public recep
 tion\, and are encouraged to propose class-specific programmes or communit
 y workshops. Promotional support includes an annual gallery catalogue\, po
 stcards\, press releases and web and social coverage. Applications require
  10 to 15 digital images (JPG only\, max 4 MB each)\, an image list with d
 imensions and medium\, a CV and an artist statement.\n\nOrganisation: The 
 Gallery at Penn College (Pennsylvania College of Technology)\nLocation: Wi
 lliamsport\, Pennsylvania\, USA\, on the Pennsylvania College of Technolog
 y campus. Travel note: Williamsport Regional Airport connects only to Wash
 ington Dulles\, with no other direct flights or trains\, so international 
 travel involves a connection. No nationality restriction is stated.\nAward
 : Housing\, meals\, a solo exhibition honorarium\, and a shipping and trav
 el stipend are all provided. Specific figures are not published in the cal
 l. No application fee is stated.\nDuration: Exhibition slot within the 202
 7-28 season\; review takes place during autumn semester with artists notif
 ied in January\n\nMore: https://www.pct.edu/gallery/exhibitions-entry-form
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.pct.edu/gallery/exhibitions-entry-form?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Williamsport\, Pennsylvania\, USA\, on the Pennsylvania College of
  Technology campus. Travel note: Williamsport Regional Airport connects on
 ly to Washington Dulles\, with no other direct flights or trains\, so inte
 rnational travel involves a connection. No nationality restriction is stat
 ed.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:albee-foundation-barn-residency-winter-spring-2027@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260914
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:Deadline: Edward F. Albee Foundation: William Flanagan Memorial Cre
 ative Persons Center Residency (Winter/Spring 2027)
DESCRIPTION:Edward Albee founded this foundation in 1962 with the proceeds 
 from Who's Afraid of Virginia Woolf?\, and it has run The Barn in Montauk 
 for close to sixty years as a place for writers and visual artists to get 
 uninterrupted time. The house sits on a secluded knoll and takes four resi
 dents at a time\, usually two writers and two visual artists\, each with a
 n en-suite bedroom and a separate studio. The environment is deliberately 
 simple and communal: residents share responsibility for the space and are 
 expected to work seriously and keep it quiet for everyone else. ELIGIBILIT
 Y: any creative person over 18\, any nationality\, students included\; sel
 ection uses talent and need as the only criteria. There are no fees at all
 \, to apply or to stay. Domestic Fellows get a USD 2\,500 stipend on compl
 eting the residency\; international Fellows get expenses reimbursed up to 
 USD 2\,500. DATES: this cycle covers residencies from January to June 2027
 . Applications open at 08:00 on 17 August 2026 and close on 14 September 2
 026\, through apply.albeefoundation.org. The next cycle (July-November 202
 7) opens 11 January 2027 and closes 15 February 2027. The Barn is closed e
 very December. Contact apply@albeefoundation.org or (212) 226-2020.\n\nOrg
 anisation: The Edward F. Albee Foundation\nLocation: Montauk\, Long Island
 \, New York\, USA (about two miles from downtown Montauk and the Atlantic)
 \nAward: Fully funded residency: private en-suite bedroom and separate wor
 king studio\, USD 2\,500 stipend for domestic Fellows paid on successful c
 ompletion\, or reimbursement of expenses capped at USD 2\,500 for internat
 ional Fellows. No application fee and no residency fee.\nDuration: One ful
 l calendar month between January and June 2027\; partial months are not of
 fered\n\nMore: https://apply.albeefoundation.org/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://apply.albeefoundation.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Montauk\, Long Island\, New York\, USA (about two miles from downt
 own Montauk and the Atlantic)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:abbey-foundation-day-use-studio-grant-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261201
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261202
SUMMARY:Deadline: Abbey Foundation for the Arts: Day Use Studio Grant (Jan 
 to Jun 2027)
DESCRIPTION:The Abbey Foundation for the Arts in Boonville\, California awa
 rds six-month day-use studio residencies paired with a materials stipend o
 f up to USD 5\,000 for equipment and supplies needed to realise the propos
 ed project. The studio is 850 square feet indoors with an additional 850 s
 quare foot screened outdoor area for large-scale work\; note that it may b
 e shared with one other artist during some periods. ELIGIBILITY: open to a
 rtists across all mediums. IMPORTANT: this is a day-use studio grant\, not
  a live-in residency\, and the foundation provides no support for housing\
 , transportation\, meals or travel\, so it states the grant is most approp
 riate for artists already living in the local counties. CONDITIONS: at the
  end of the residency the artist is expected to contribute one of the piec
 es made while in residence to the foundation\, and the programme may come 
 to require grant recipients to take part in community workshops as it deve
 lops. DEADLINES: 1 December 2026 for the 1 January to 30 June 2027 residen
 cy\; the foundation runs the call twice a year\, with a 1 June deadline fo
 r the following July to December period. No application fee.\n\nOrganisati
 on: Abbey Foundation for the Arts\nLocation: Boonville\, California\, Unit
 ed States (Anderson Valley\, Mendocino County). Day use of the studio only
 : there is NO support for housing\, transportation\, meals or travel\, so 
 the foundation says it suits artists already living in the surrounding cou
 nties.\nAward: Stipend of up to USD 5\,000 for the purchase of materials a
 nd additional equipment needed to complete the proposed project\, plus six
  months of day use of an 850 square foot studio and a further 850 square f
 oot screened outdoor area for large-scale work. No housing\, travel\, meal
 s or transport support. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-month residency
 \, 1 January to 30 June 2027\n\nMore: https://www.abbeyfoundationfortheart
 s.org/grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.abbeyfoundationforthearts.org/grants?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Boonville\, California\, United States (Anderson Valley\, Mendocin
 o County). Day use of the studio only: there is NO support for housing\, t
 ransportation\, meals or travel\, so the foundation says it suits artists 
 already living in the surrounding counties.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mad-artist-studios-residency-cycle-46-47@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260911
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260912
SUMMARY:Deadline: MAD Artist Studios Residency\, Cycles 46 and 47
DESCRIPTION:Established in 2008 and the first of its kind\, the Artist Stud
 ios at MAD is a public-facing community engagement and professional develo
 pment programme on the sixth floor of the Museum\, where visitors meet art
 ists-in-residence at work. Two artists are selected per six-month session 
 (the application text elsewhere says three\, so confirm with MAD). More th
 an 200 artists and designers have come through the programme\, which MAD d
 escribes as an innovative model for museum education\, with a sustained fo
 cus on artists from historically underrepresented communities whose work p
 ushes the boundaries of contemporary craft\; alumni have exhibited at the 
 Queens Museum\, Whitney\, Brooklyn Museum and Studio Museum. WHAT MAD MEAN
 S BY CRAFT: both a discipline and a way of thinking\, covering traditional
  studio craft materials such as clay\, fiber\, wood\, glass\, metal and st
 one\, and craft-based methods used in new ways\, including handwork combin
 ed with digital tools\, performance or experimental materials. Craft here 
 is defined by deep engagement with process\, materiality and the hand rath
 er than by medium. RESIDENCY EXPECTATIONS: this is a substantial public co
 mmitment\, not quiet studio time. Residents commit to 14 or 21 public open
  studio hours a week on Thursdays\, Fridays and/or Saturdays 10:30am to 5:
 30pm and/or Wednesdays 11:00am to 7:00pm\, on a schedule fixed with MAD's 
 Education Department for the whole six months. During those hours resident
 s host K-12 school groups (in person and virtual) and public tour groups. 
 Because of the contact with schoolchildren\, all selected artists must pas
 s a background check. ELIGIBILITY: an artist or designer with an active st
 udio practice in the applied arts\; a resident of the New York City metro 
 area\, defined as the five boroughs plus nearby New York\, New Jersey and 
 Connecticut counties within reasonable commuting distance\, typically on M
 TA\, PATH or NJ Transit\; at least 21 at the time of application\; and a U
 S citizen or legal US resident with a valid SSN or EIN. The Museum does no
 t sponsor visas and states this is not subject to exception. Programme alu
 mni may not reapply\, though unsuccessful past applicants may. SELECTION: 
 the committee looks for a mature body of work engaging critically with the
  transdisciplinary fields of contemporary craft\, design\, art and technol
 ogy\, a strong conceptual framework\, deep engagement with process and mat
 erials\, a clear direction or question for the residency\, and genuine rea
 diness for the open studios format. Top applications go to interview with 
 the Deputy Director of Education and Manager of Public Programs and Studio
 s\, and finalists' references are contacted. Applicants are strongly encou
 raged to visit the studios in person first. NOT AN EXHIBITION PROGRAMME: r
 esidents may show work in progress in their studio and finished work in th
 e sixth-floor vitrines\, but the residency does not include a gallery exhi
 bition or a proposal for MAD's galleries. STUDIO RESTRICTIONS: no open fla
 mes\, all hot work approved by the Director of Facilities\, no large or un
 safe power tools such as saws\, and no toxic materials including turpentin
 e\, certain resins\, glazes\, lusters and lead-based paints. Applicants su
 bmit one application indicating their preferred schedule and which session
  or sessions they want\, and are selected for one. Initial notifications O
 ctober 2026\, finalist interviews November 2026\, offers December 2026. Qu
 estions: education@madmuseum.org. Deadline 11 September 2026.\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Museum of Arts and Design (MAD)\nLocation: Sixth floor of the Museum 
 of Arts and Design\, New York City\, USA. Open to residents of the New Yor
 k City metro area only.\nAward: Stipend based on the on-site public schedu
 le: USD 15\,000 for three public-facing days per week\, or USD 10\,000 for
  two days. Also provided: a dedicated studio of roughly 200 square feet in
  the Museum with worktable\, sink\, storage and Wi-Fi\; a separate stipend
  for materials and mentorship\; professional development and studio visits
  with museum staff\; additional paid public programming opportunities\; ad
 mission to paid public programmes\; and access to MAD's galleries\, public
  programmes and exhibition openings. Shared equipment includes a Skutt 102
 7 kiln\, a Brent pottery wheel and a 22 inch floor loom. MAD does not pay 
 travel costs or provide housing. Residents are one-time independent contra
 ctors and are responsible for their own taxes. Collectives may apply but a
 re paid the individual stipend. APPLICATION FEE: USD 5 per application\, w
 ith fee waivers available on request by emailing education@madmuseum.org b
 efore submitting.\nDuration: Six months. Cycle 46 runs 15 February 2027 to
  15 August 2027\; Cycle 47 runs 16 August 2027 to 13 February 2028.\n\nMor
 e: https://madmuseum.submittable.com/submit/363177/mad-artist-studios-arti
 st-residency-cycle-46-and-47?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
URL:https://madmuseum.submittable.com/submit/363177/mad-artist-studios-arti
 st-residency-cycle-46-and-47?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Sixth floor of the Museum of Arts and Design\, New York City\, USA
 . Open to residents of the New York City metro area only.
END:VEVENT
